# Normalise line endings so a Windows checkout is usable by the Linux sandbox.
#
# The host shell scripts (`dev-entrypoint.sh`, `scripts/*.sh`) are executed
# INSIDE the Linux container through the bind mount. With `core.autocrlf=true`
# (the Git for Windows default) they land in the working tree with CRLF, and
# the kernel then reports:
#
#     /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# or `$'\r': command not found` on every line. `text=auto eol=lf` keeps the
# index and the working tree LF on every platform, so a Windows clone produces
# the same bytes as a Linux one. Do not relax this to `eol=crlf` for scripts.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be normalised.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.webm  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary

# Windows entry points may use CRLF without affecting anything: they are read
# by PowerShell, not by the Linux kernel.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
